WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.480
Speaker 1: Earlier in the show I told you
all of the ways in which this protracted fiasco

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:09.630
in the House of Representatives wherein Republicans
have been unable to elect a speaker is bad

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:10.630
for Republicans.

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:11.630
Now, that's good.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:12.630
That makes me happy.

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:13.780
I think the Republican Party is terrible for
the country.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:15.490
I think their goals and ambitions are destructive.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:19.580
I want them to have a quagmire and to be unable
to push forward their disgusting agenda.

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:22.320
However, I don't want the country to fail.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:27.410
And as a general principle, not having a House
speaker is bad for the country and it's a

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:33.530
national security risk and it is a source
of instability on the bulwarks.

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:36.480
Joe per token Republican wrote an article
touching on some of these issues called The

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.940
House is Paralyzed without a speaker creating
national security risks and a shadow shut

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:40.940
down.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:41.940
Imagine if this impasse were occurring in
a presidential inaugural year.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.100
And Joe, very precisely and astutely points
out all of the different ways in which this

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.410
current status quo is very bad, including
that no one has actually been sworn in to

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:53.989
the House of Representatives yet, even though
George Santo said he was sworn in, he wasn't.

00:00:53.989 --> 00:00:56.620
And we'll get to that a little bit later.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:01.230
And I think it's important just to be clear
about the risks that this presents.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.739
It is, I would argue, even simpler than Joe
Per Tacconi lays out.

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:07.170
It's not that he's wrong, but it's even simpler.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:11.540
This situation in the House where no one has
been sworn in and there is no speaker, it

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:16.950
can lead to a functional breakdown of the
House of Representatives.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:21.060
The speaker actually plays a critical role
in managing and organizing the legislative

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.290
process and with no speaker.

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:26.061
How does the House conduct business and pass
legislation?

00:01:26.061 --> 00:01:27.260
Is it possible to do?

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.590
It is, but it's significantly more of a mess
within government.

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:31.690
No speaker and no one sworn in.

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:32.690
Causes uncertainty.

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:33.690
It causes instability.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:37.670
The speaker is one of the most powerful and
influential figures or positions in Congress,

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:39.560
and the American confidence in government
is certain to decline.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:42.689
The our perception of the ability of the House
to govern is quite low, accurately, because

00:01:42.689 --> 00:01:43.689
this is what they're doing.

00:01:43.689 --> 00:01:44.909
And then you say, okay, fine.

00:01:44.909 --> 00:01:46.149
That's that's that's abstract.

00:01:46.149 --> 00:01:48.369
But in practical terms, what could that mean?

00:01:48.369 --> 00:01:51.820
Well, it can lead to gridlock or inaction
on important issues.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:57.159
When you think about debt ceiling votes or
if there is a natural disaster of some kind,

00:01:57.159 --> 00:02:01.540
a crisis, an emergency, the need for funding
in a specific place, way or form without a

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:05.950
speaker and without a sworn in house, it is
significantly more difficult for the House

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:10.759
to actually manage those things, to respond
to unforeseen events.

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:17.580
And at the end of the day, to protect the
country's interests.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.401
Now, do I think that Republicans don't care
about that?

00:02:20.401 --> 00:02:22.600
No, I think at the individual level, a lot
of these Republicans would honestly say, no,

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:24.170
I do care about that.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.129
I understand that I'm concerned.

00:02:26.129 --> 00:02:37.940
Does that mean they should compromise on their
principles to the extent that this situation

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:39.780
is caused by or catalyzed by their principles?

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:40.960
No, I'm not saying that.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.250
But we have to be factual.

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:43.580
We have to be honest.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:45.530
We have to be realistic.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:57.440
The longer this goes on, the more instability
is accurately perceived by the American people.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.770
And obviously, the longer it goes on, the
higher the chances are that something could

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:06.480
happen domestically, internationally, and
in other ways that would require, in normal

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:13.760
circumstances, the involvement of the House
of Representatives.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:19.610
And they will then be chaotically scrambling
to try to get involved, help or do something

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:20.610
about it.

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:21.610
So.

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:22.610
The primary interest from.

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:25.360
We have we have conflicting interests here.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:35.920
Each faction of the Republican Party wants
to reign supreme in terms of ultimately deciding

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:41.110
who is going to become speaker of the House.

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:44.709
I get that.

00:03:44.709 --> 00:03:45.709
That's politics.

00:03:45.709 --> 00:03:51.470
They don't want to be seen as the losers.

00:03:51.470 --> 00:04:04.549
They don't want to be seen as the ones who
were impotent in trying to impose their preference

00:04:04.549 --> 00:04:06.400
of speaker.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:09.189
That's one area.

00:04:09.189 --> 00:04:18.019
The impact on the Republican Party we've already
talked about.

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:36.120
On the other hand, I hope that I'm accurate
in assessing that the 

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:51.410
interest of the American people is to not
have to think about the House of Representatives.

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:57.600
And what I mean by that is, yes, there are
some people who are more politically engaged

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.289
than others who pay attention to.

00:05:01.289 --> 00:05:02.289
When are the midterms?

00:05:02.289 --> 00:05:06.040
Where is control of the Senate?

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:11.410
Where is control of the House and are more
involved in that, including people like those

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:15.520
who listen to this program, who watch our
clips?

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:29.220
The vast majority of the country, even many
of those who vote in the midterms, don't spend

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:32.169
their days and nights thinking about this
stuff.

00:05:32.169 --> 00:05:36.720
And so it's sort of like referees in a in
a sporting event, if you're noticing the referees,

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:39.811
if you're thinking about the referees, if
the referees are significantly present in

00:05:39.811 --> 00:05:47.080
the game, arguably it is because they are
doing something wrong or too involved or whatever

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:48.550
the case may be.

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:56.130
You've heard these sports analogies before
and likewise, I would argue if the American

00:05:56.130 --> 00:06:08.810
people are thinking about the chaos in the
House of Representatives to the extent that

00:06:08.810 --> 00:06:21.360
it has been front and center for the last
two or three days, it is a it is a bad thing.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:28.680
It is the type of thing where we shouldn't
really have to be thinking about it.

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:37.330
The house 
is there.

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:40.770
There's a natural disaster.

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:42.910
They're going to act.

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:47.770
They're going to they're going to help.

00:06:47.770 --> 00:07:02.169
They're going to fund.

00:07:02.169 --> 00:07:05.360
And that's where the national security risk
comes here.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:17.720
We now have a balancing, a coming together
of how long are they willing to continue this

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:24.000
and how tolerant will the American people
be before approval rating, not only for the

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:33.759
House as a body where the approval rating
is always low, but approval for their individual

00:07:33.759 --> 00:07:39.940
members of Congress is going to plummet?

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:53.060
I don't know the answer to that, but I'm guessing
we're going to start finding out about that

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:55.259
very, very soon.

